# Node / Express — project structure

Canonical server tree. Do not put route handlers, SQL, and validation in one mega-file.

```
server/
  index.ts                 # bootstrap: create app, mount routers, listen
  app.ts                   # optional: express() + middleware wiring
  config/                  # env, database pool, constants
  middleware/              # auth, error handler, validateRequest
  routes/                  # HTTP only — thin controllers
  services/                # business logic (no req/res)
  repositories/            # or queries/ — DB access only
  validation/              # Zod (or project) schemas
  types/
  utils/                   # logger, helpers (no domain logic)
```

## Layering

```
routes → services → repositories/queries → DB
         ↘ validation / middleware
```

- **routes**: parse input, authz, call service, map status. No raw SQL.
- **services**: business rules / transactions. No `req`/`res`.
- **repositories**: parameterized SQL / ORM only. No HTTP.

| Kind | Put it in |
|------|-----------|
| HTTP endpoint | `server/routes/<domain>.ts` |
| Business rule | `server/services/` |
| SQL / persistence | `server/repositories/` or `queries/` |
| Request schema | `server/validation/` |
| Auth / guards | `server/middleware/` |

## Hard rules

- No `server/helpers.ts` dumping grounds — name by domain.
- Do not import DB drivers in routes; go through config + repository.
- Match existing names (`queries/` vs `repositories/`) when already consistent.
- One domain per route file; split around ~200–300 lines.
